 ## Overview
-Dynamic Resolution Adaptation is a runtime performance management technique that adjusts the render viewport reduction factor based on previous frame render times and an allocated time budget to maintain a target frame rate [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of performance instability in volume rendering, where small parametric changes (e.g., transfer function modifications) can cause frame rate dips leading to cybersickness [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from static resolution schemes by continuously shifting the "anchor points" between resolution levels based on the gradient of recent frame times, rather than using fixed blending factors [S1, unofficial].
+Dynamic Resolution Adaptation (DRA) is a runtime performance management technique that adjusts the render viewport reduction factor based on previous frame render time and an allocated time budget to maintain a target frame rate [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of performance instability in volume rendering, where small parametric changes (such as transfer function modifications) can cause frame rate dips leading to cybersickness [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from static resolution schemes by continuously shifting the "anchor points" between resolution levels based on the gradient of recent frame times, rather than using fixed blending factors [S1, unofficial].
 
